year  since uh  what palestinians have often referred to  as the unity intifada since a an  outbreak an escalation  that started in jerusalem so israel  would be very well advised uh not  to  impose a heavy not to have a heavy  footprint uh in its um security and  policing arrangements uh in jerusalem  over the next period and of course we've  also seen uh  chef jaraka the evictions of palestinian  families in that sensitive spot uh in  jerusalem so  we don't know yet how different this is  one thing we have seen  is  that the first two attacks were claimed  uh by daesh  involved palestinian citizens of israel  uh yasser referred to the conditions of  structural discrimination and inequality  uh in which those citizens live it's a  it's a  it's a strange time almost for that  community when you have a party in the  government representing that community  a lot of resentment also uh towards that  so we don't know how different it's  going to be what we do know mohammed is  after a long period of rule under  netanyahu this is the first time we're  seeing this kind of hybrid bennett  coalition um  dealing with  with this and i think there is genuine  concern  that if bennett were to pursue the  extreme rhetoric  that he voiced in the past as his actual  policy response then he's lighting a  match then things will get even uh  more deeply into a spiral would be the  expectation so i think you've got you've  got a spectrum right you could have a  very  um ill-advised  very securitized israeli response you  could have business as usual which is  also pretty bad or you could have  something which  i fear is not even on the agenda which  is to say yup we've got to get to terms  with the immediacy immediacy of this but  maybe this is the moment to address the  deeper political grievance  and if we don't address that there's the  inevitability of more with that is not a  narrative one's hearing so i think this  will be a test for bennett and of course  what you will have  is a a very agitating  uh right wing opposition outside of the  government that will be  paying for blood to to use that term  yes  prime minister bennett said that we will  fight terror with perseverance  stubbornness and an iron fist so my  first question to you is what kind of a  response do you expect will be seen  from israel my second question to you is  are you also concerned that there will  be more attacks that the attacks that  have happened thus far will inspire more  attacks especially at such a fraught  time  well i mean in terms of israeli response  the kind of response that we have seen  over the years and the past decades has  has always  been about using military power against  the palestinians uh so um that has been  always the israeli response  and it's a response that military  response for me does not represent a  solution it represents a problem and and  it causes more problems on the ground  and because let's let's not forget that  the military response is also part of  the occupation structure and the israeli  military is in the palestinian  territories and it's  on a daily basis  it's the the israeli military is engaged  in  military action against the palestinians  is engaged in violence against the  palestinians so i think we need to put  that in context not to defend violence  violence is not acceptable of course on  any side and  or  you know at the international level as  well but really to understand this long  palestinian experience of living under  occupation basically what they've been  facing is a systematic system of  apartheid a systematic system of  occupation that has all this system has  always presented to the palestinians not  a deeper political response as your  speaker referred to but rather a  military response and that military  response for me is about increasing  injustice on the ground it's about also  increasing tension on the ground what we  need is a broader political and deeper  solution that would deal with the root  issues um  and that and those issues include  security of course and they include also  palestinian rights and we have to  recognize that now  you referred also in your question  to the israeli prime minister planet  um bennett is on record um saying that  he is opposed to palestinian rights he  is against palestinian rights he is  against palestinian statehood so i'm not  expecting a political response if you  like  from bennett i'm expecting a hard and  extreme right-wing response  um  and that's not going to help that it's  going to increase the tension and the  injustice on the ground well and  and it's a response as i said that  israel has tried in the past and the  problem hasn't gone away so that means  we need to try something  more just we need to strive something  that is more comprehensive and something  that will address the problem and in my  view what will address the problem is a  deeper political solution that will  facilitate palestinian rights and  security for all  you'll see is the fact that the  religious holidays of ramadan passover  and easter the fact that they're going  to be converging this year is that  something that's also leading to an  escalation of tensions  probably and their expectation of some  some increase in violence i don't think  at this level in in this sense i think  the security services here were  surprised  but interestingly enough the three  incidents though they have some  commonality the source of it are quite  different  the first incidence in beersheba was  actually carried out by by bedwin  the second one is actually from benos  from um which is a stronghold of the  islamist movement and the third one is  actually from the palestinians from in  the occupied territories from  janine from yavin so i think  we are trying to connect all the three  of us and no doubts you know that the  the conditions on the ground and the  lack of hope and horizon that was  mentioned  off of peace were mentioned by the other  speakers play a part but i think we  still need to there are no doubt  triggers that for us those of us who  deal day in day out with with this  conflict  we are not surprised not because we  would like to see violence is because we  keep warning that without a  comprehensive solution for the israeli  palestinian conflict violence is is  almost inevitable  and the the inability of the leadership  to do anything about  about this and then blame everyone when  it does happen is is is the waterfall of  it reality is enough to either to visit  the west bank and to see the complete  apathy  on the other side of the green line when  it comes to the life of palestinians and  saying there is a  nato divide and then the international  community is is completely  disinterested and that's sadly those are  the reminders now as for how israel is  going to react sadly i think  this kind of situation there will be  overreaction you you you won't expect  the israeli security forces especially  this sensitive time of the holidays not  to respond to this because  as  as i said myself here in right now in  tel aviv you don't know whether god  forbid is the next dex attack so you  will see  the presence of more security forces in  the streets  but the question if there is an ability  to move away just from security response  from using more force and then there  will be more palestinian victims as a  result of it and probably on the other  side too or to do something else the sad  reality for just may end is on both  sides there are very weak administration  very weak governments and we governments  tend to overreact instead of actually  being reflective  daniel it looked like you wanted to jump  in i'm going to let you do that but but  i also want to ask you um  what i seem to be hearing in one form or  another from from you from yossi and  from yasir is that really things aren't  going to change unless there's a real  look at the root causes of what's going  on and an attempt at a comprehensive  solution so let me ask you is there any  hope right now that that is something  that can actually be achieved  well i i i unfortunately can can give  you a relatively categorical and clear  response on on that last point which is  no we're not in the solution zone  there is  no willingness on the israeli side to  end  the occupation to end what human rights  organizations have designated as a  reality of apartheid  in the system which uh which prevails  and you have and this is this is also  a a a significant problem you have a  palestinian authority  that  has  distanced itself so much  from its people that has not allowed  itself to be held accountable by its  people in elections etc and that is very  widely viewed as a subcontractor  for the israeli authorities and is  therefore not in a position to rally its  people in in one direction or another  but where i wanted to to to intervene  specifically is  is that  if there is this overreaction if we now  do get into a cycle what what we've seen  in these three attacks  indiscriminate on civilians  categorically  unacceptable but once you get into a  broader escalation then one has to be  reminded of the fact that palestinians  have a legitimate right and even  responsibility to resist this military  occupation and i say that in the context  of the daily commentary and pictures  that we see coming out of ukraine and  suddenly when people are  stockpiling molotov cocktails they are  being lauded as heroic courageous  patriots if you do that while  palestinian  you are almost always declared a  terrorist  all right well we have run out of time  so we're going to have to leave the  conversation there thank you so much to  all of our guests you'll see mecklenburg  and daniel levy  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com and  for further discussion go to our  facebook page that's facebook.com  forward slash aj inside story you can  also join the conversation on twitter  our handle is at aj inside story for me  muhammad jim jroom and the whole team  here in doha bye for now  [Music]
 

